WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:12.500
If you have recently been treated for a heart attack a condition caused by a blockage of blood flow to your heart muscle

00:00:12.809 --> 00:00:16.668
This video will help you understand the condition and its treatment

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:23.869
Your heart is a muscular organ that pumps blood containing the oxygen and nutrients your body needs

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.949
The main pumping chamber of your heart is the left ventricle

00:00:28.679 --> 00:00:36.378
When your left ventricle contracts it sends oxygen-rich blood to your body through a large artery called the aorta

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:41.690
Connected to your aorta our small arteries called coronary arteries

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:52.760
Blood flows from your aorta through the coronary arteries to supply your heart muscle with oxygen and nutrients

00:00:56.450 --> 00:01:04.059
During your heart attack blood flow through one of your coronary arteries may have been severely reduced or completely blocked

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:11.679
Your reduced blood flow may have been caused by a buildup of a fatty substance called plaque in your coronary arteries

00:01:13.409 --> 00:01:15.159
If this plaque became

00:01:15.159 --> 00:01:21.599
Disrupted a blood clot might form and severely worsen the narrowing or lead to a sudden complete blockage

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:24.210
Stopping blood flow down the artery

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:28.880
a blockage in your coronary arteries

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:35.389
prevented the oxygen and nutrients in your blood from reaching that part of your heart supplied by the artery as

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:39.349
A result heart muscle in that area started to die

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:44.289
Damage to part of your heart muscle is called a heart attack

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:49.509
It's also known as a myocardial infarction or MI

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:58.419
Your heart doctor may have recommended a procedure to help open the blockage and improve blood flow to the damaged area

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:05.659
The procedure you had may have been a coronary angioplasty

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:14.630
During a coronary angioplasty a balloon tipped catheter inflates inside your blocked coronary artery to open it

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:20.970
The procedure may have involved placing a stent to help prop the artery open

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:25.049
This is usually a thin metal mesh that acts as a scaffold

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:32.140
Or you may have had a coronary artery, bypass graft or CABG

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:39.000
CABG is a surgical procedure in which the blocked areas of the coronary arteries are

00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:42.059
Bypassed with veins or other arteries from the body

00:02:45.969 --> 00:02:52.019
Before you left the hospital your healthcare provider most likely prescribed several medications

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:57.420
Your medication may include the following

00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:00.990
Oral antiplatelet therapy

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:06.149
Helps prevent platelets from sticking together and forming new blood clots

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:15.419
You may have also received drugs called beta blockers that help lower your heart rate and blood pressure

00:03:18.490 --> 00:03:20.200
Drugs such as

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:23.310
angiotensin converting enzyme or ACE inhibitors

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:27.510
angiotensin receptor blockers or ARBs and

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:32.789
calcium channel blockers also work to lower your blood pressure, if needed and

00:03:33.459 --> 00:03:38.699
You may have been prescribed statins along with a low-fat diet to lower your cholesterol

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:44.159
These drugs work by reducing the amount of cholesterol made in your liver

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:54.690
It is important to stay on your medications as your physician prescribed even if you are feeling better

00:03:55.330 --> 00:04:01.050
Do not go off your medication unless the healthcare professional that prescribed them tells you to

